<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohechío</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1500</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohechío was the paramount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> of Jaragua in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived, ruling from a town near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leogane">Léogâne</a> and receiving tribute from many lesser chiefs. He received Bartholomew Columbus in 1497 and agreed to pay tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> bread rather than gold, which his land lacked. On his death his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacaona">Anacaona</a> succeeded him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its paramount cacique <b>Bohechío</b> ruled from a town near modern Léogâne, receiving tribute from many lesser chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-jaragua">Cacicazgo of Jaragua</a></li><li><blockquote>His wife Anacaona was the sister of <b>Bohechío</b> of Jaragua, linking the two chiefdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-maguana">Cacicazgo of Maguana</a></li></ul></section>
